Docker Engine and container tools

Install:
  Install missing Docker packages and NVIDIA container support when an NVIDIA GPU is present.
  Configure Docker group access, an optional local daemon proxy and automatic build cache GC, including when Docker is already installed.
  Enable and restart a newly installed Docker Engine; preserve an existing engine's service settings.

Upgrade:
  Update installed Docker and NVIDIA container packages; preserve daemon proxy and service settings.
  Apply build cache GC settings when Docker Engine is installed, preparing missing configuration tools as needed.

Status:
  Report package versions, Docker service activity and startup state, daemon proxy presence and build cache GC file settings.

Requirements:
  Ubuntu, APT, a running systemd instance and root or sudo access.

Configuration:
  Daemon proxy configuration: /etc/systemd/system/docker.service.d/wsley-proxy.conf
  Daemon configuration: /etc/docker/daemon.json
  Build cache GC applies to the default docker driver, with a 100GB cleanup threshold; stricter existing limits remain effective, and separate BuildKit builders use their own configuration.
  Default policies retain existing space targets where they fit the limit; custom policies retain their order and conditions and receive an all-cache 100GB rule when absent.
  GC also considers cache age; the threshold is not a hard disk quota.
  The optional daemon proxy uses HTTP at 127.0.0.1 and preserves an existing proxy file. Leave the port empty for 7897, enter 0 to skip or enter a port from 1 to 65535; --yes does not answer this prompt.
  The proxy applies to daemon image pulls; container proxy settings and the upstream proxy listener are administrator-managed.
  Existing runtime configuration is preserved; a missing daemon file receives NVIDIA runtime settings when NVIDIA container support is newly installed.
  Daemon configuration is validated before replacement. Restart an existing Docker Engine to apply proxy, GPU runtime or GC changes; the configuration step does not restart it.
  Status reads configuration files; use docker buildx inspect default after restarting to inspect active GC policies.
  Install adds the invoking user to the Docker group; sudo uses the original user, and direct root installation skips group membership. Group access grants root-equivalent privileges; log out and back in after a membership change.
